This file describes the modifications and additions (and subtractions) tothe original KA9Q/K5JB v37 package.	OSKNET provides TCP/IP connetions via asyncronous serial connectionsusing SLIP or PPP protocols. Van Jacobsen compression is supported byboth protocols. Password Authentication is available for PPP connectionsand Challenge Authentication is in the works. OSKNET can be used forInternet access through a provider, but only telnet and FTP are availableat this time. It is hoped that some (other) enterprising person(s) willbe willing to port a WEB-browser for this package.	The executable OSKNET was compiled on an AT306 with 8meg of RAM,using the Microware 'C' compiler v3.1, and should run on any box withOSK.	You will need to set the Environment Variables:			NETHOME		to your OSKNET files dir - mine is /dd/net 			NETSPOOL	to the spooler dir - mine is /dd/net/spool	The included directory NET includes examples of the necissary files anddirectories.	 	The original documentation in /NET95/DOC is very extensive, but, afew differences should be noted. The biggest change is that the serialport speed is now changed according to the 'attach' command in 'startup.okn'.See the file /NET95/DOC/useguide.doc and the commented /NET/startup.okn filefor more info on the attach command. Note that ppp protocol has been added,but, the syntax has not changed. For more information on the PPP options,see the file /NET/DOC/ppp.doc.	All of the Radio functions have been removed from OSKNET as it wasfelt that users who wished to use radio would be better served by one ofthe popular NOS (JNOS, TNOS, etc) packages available. OSKNET is more foruse over serial lines either to an internet provider, or as a local networkserved by a multi-port server. My home network consists of an MM/1 and aCoco (!) with an AT306 as a server and internet gateway, all running OS9.	The radio functions could easily be added to OSKNET from the enclosedoriginal source files, as the structures and naming conventions have beenmaintained.	The simple (stupid?) dialer is new to OSKNET, so a brief explanationis in order. To invoke the dialer, type: 'dial <interface name> <file>'.The dialer only recognizes two commands, send & wait. Send sends the rest of the line out to the serial port used by the named interface. Andwaits for a string from the serial port that matches the rest of it's line.To dial my server at work via the serial port that was 'attach'ed to theinterface named 'pp4' using the included 'work.dial' file, I type:'dial pp4 work.dial'. This will be clearer after you read the NET95 docs.